title: "Code Review AI: can an LLM review pull requests better than a trained model?"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Melis
    given-names: Marlen
    affiliation: KAIST
version: 1.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-25"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/MarlenMM/code-review-ai"
keywords:
  - code review
  - mining software repositories
  - pull requests
  - merge prediction
  - large language models
  - prompt engineering
  - AI-generated code
  - VS Code extension
abstract: >-
  Four experiments on 1,494 closed pull requests mined from five large open-source repositories
  (microsoft/vscode, dotnet/runtime, dotnet/aspnetcore, microsoft/semantic-kernel, home-assistant/core),
  25.4% of them authored by an AI coding agent: leakage-free merge prediction with SVM and Random Forest
  models, a 4 x 4 grid of LLM context levels and prompt strategies on human-written code, a 5 x 5 grid on
  AI-generated code, and a FastAPI backend with a VS Code extension that combines the trained model and
  the best LLM configuration. Built for Project II (AI-powered Code Review) of the HIT Global Summer
  School, July 2026.
